    "body": "**Before:** after `sp-load-theme` to a theme with another background, white borders stayed around the buffers. `sp-windows` painted the window dividers, the fringe and (indirectly) the frame border with the default background **once, when the module loaded**, so they kept the colour of the theme loaded at startup. Most themes (Doric included) also give `internal-border` no background, and Emacs does not repaint a frame's border when a face changes.\n\n**After:** `sp-windows-blend-borders` paints the three divider faces, `fringe`, `internal-border` and `child-frame-border` with the current default background and redraws the graphic frames. It runs at load, from `sp-after-load-theme-hook`, and for each new graphic frame (a daemon that started without a frame has no background to read yet). Values like `unspecified-bg` (terminal frames) are skipped.\n\n**Checked** (Emacs 29.3, no GUI available): with the default background stubbed to two colours in turn, all five faces follow it. **Not checked:** a real graphic frame, so please confirm on your Mac that the borders now follow `sp-load-theme`. If a border is still white, it is probably the `spacious-padding` faces, and I would look there next.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_013wuoefeVHQQNwZgJptsdeH)_",
